-- title:
--   Ideal sheaf inclusion from germs at specialisations
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and let $I$ and $J$ be two pieces of ideal sheaf data on $X$, that is, quasi-coherent ideal sheaves recorded by their ideals $I.\mathrm{ideal}\,U \subseteq \Gamma(U,\mathcal O_X)$ indexed by the affine opens $U$ of $X$. Assume that for every point $x$ of $X$ lying in the support of $J$ there exist a point $y$ of $X$ with $x \rightsquigarrow y$ (so $y$ lies in the closure of $\{x\}$), an affine open $U \subseteq X$ and a proof that $y \in U$, such that the ideal of $\mathcal O_{X,y}$ generated by the image of $I.\mathrm{ideal}\,U$ under the germ ring homomorphism $\Gamma(U,\mathcal O_X) \to \mathcal O_{X,y}$ is contained in the ideal generated by the image of $J.\mathrm{ideal}\,U$ under the same germ map. The conclusion is $I \le J$ in the order on ideal sheaf data, i.e. $I.\mathrm{ideal}\,U \subseteq J.\mathrm{ideal}\,U$ for every affine open $U$. Note that the hypothesis is only imposed at points of the support of $J$, and only at one specialisation $y$ of each such point, with one affine open containing it.
--
--   This is a local-to-global criterion for an inclusion of quasi-coherent ideal sheaves on a scheme: the inclusion may be tested on germs, and only at a single specialisation of each point of the support of the larger ideal sheaf. It is used in the Drinfeld-type global Weierstrass constructions, in the step bounding a torsion ideal sheaf by a basis divisor.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_IdealSheafData_le_of_forall_mem_support_exists_specializes_map_germ_le.lean

